INT. COURTHOUSE - BASEMENT BOILER ROOM - NIGHT

Pipes the width of tree trunks. A hissing furnace. A single caged bulb. On a workbench: a jar of coffee and a radio playing low jazz.

ATTORNEY GENEVIEVE KOWALSKI-BEAUFORT (40s, wet raincoat, briefcase) picks her way between the pipes, calling softly.

GENEVIEVE
Mr. Fitch? Ephraim Fitch?

A rattle of keys. An old CUSTODIAN (60s, coveralls, ring of keys at his belt) steps from behind the boiler, wiping his hands on a rag.

CUSTODIAN
You're not allowed down here.

GENEVIEVE
I'm Genevieve Kowalski. Your daughter's attorney.

CUSTODIAN
(a pause)
Celeste has an attorney?

GENEVIEVE
She does now.

He sets the rag on the workbench, studies her, then pulls a second stool from the shadows. She sits. He pours coffee into a chipped mug and slides it across.

CUSTODIAN (CONT'D)
Black. Sorry. No cream.

GENEVIEVE
Black's fine.

The jazz plays. The furnace ticks.

GENEVIEVE (CONT'D)
Mr. Fitch, your daughter is on trial tomorrow.

EPHRAIM
I know.

GENEVIEVE
She says she was at home the night of the break-in. She says she was with you.

EPHRAIM
I'd say the same.

GENEVIEVE
Were you?

He rubs his jaw. The keys clink at his belt.

EPHRAIM
I'm the night janitor. I'm here nights.

GENEVIEVE
So you weren't with her.

EPHRAIM
No.

GENEVIEVE
Then why would she say it?

EPHRAIM
Because I told her to.

She puts down her mug. Slowly, she folds her hands.

GENEVIEVE
Mr. Fitch, I need you to repeat that very carefully.

EPHRAIM
I told her to say it. I knew the judge would believe a father.

GENEVIEVE
You knew she wasn't home.

EPHRAIM
I knew where she was.

GENEVIEVE
Where?

EPHRAIM
Here.

The caged bulb swings. Shadows lurch across the pipes. Genevieve stands.

GENEVIEVE
Here? In the courthouse?

EPHRAIM
Down here. With me. She came to fix a pipe.

GENEVIEVE
Why would she—

EPHRAIM
She's a plumber, Ms. Kowalski. She works nights. She was in the east vault, patching a leak, the night someone cleaned out the evidence room upstairs.

GENEVIEVE
So she had access.

EPHRAIM
She had a keycard I gave her.

GENEVIEVE
And you didn't tell the police.

EPHRAIM
I'm telling you.

GENEVIEVE
Why now?

EPHRAIM ^
Why now?

They stop. The radio plays a soft saxophone note. He smiles, grimly.

EPHRAIM (CONT'D)
Because the man who stole it is the one prosecuting her.

Genevieve sits down very slowly. The mug rattles on the bench.

High above, a door opens. Footsteps descend the iron stairs, unhurried. A silhouette stops at the bottom step: PROSECUTOR LEOPOLD ASHWORTH (50s, charcoal suit, cufflinks catching the bulb).

ASHWORTH
Evening, Ephraim. Visitors?

Ephraim does not turn. Genevieve slowly closes her briefcase.
